 }], "InlineSubitem",ExpressionUUID->"bf8d0c7e-0a96-b641-8165-171464eebb57"],
 "\nSo anisotropy is not just a passive descriptor; it actively modulates \
imbalance growth.\n\nIf you want, I can next give you a ",
 StyleBox["drop-in replacement for your Section 7",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " that prints the balance law in both compact and expanded form."
}], "ChatOutput",
 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "Yes \[LongDash] and the key fix is to convert the differential equations \
in ``dynamicSystem`` into actual replacement rules before substituting.\n\n## \
What went wrong\n\nYour ``dynamicSystem`` is a list of equations like\n\n\
```wl\nD[rho[x, tau], tau] == sourceOperator[x, tau]\n```\n\nbut ``/.`` \
expects rules like\n\n```wl\nD[rho[x, tau], tau] -> sourceOperator[x, tau]\n\
```\n\nSo Section 7 failed because equations are not replacement rules.\n\n\
---\n\n## Correct closed signed-density evolution\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\
\\text{signedDensity}(x,\\tau)=\\rho(x,\\tau)-\\nu(x,\\tau),\n$$\n\nwe have\n\
\n$$\n\\partial_\\tau \\text{signedDensity}\n=\n\\partial_\\tau \
\\rho-\\partial_\\tau \\nu.\n$$\n\nUsing your dynamic system,\n\n$$\n\
\\partial_\\tau \\rho\n=\n\\rho-\\nu+\\|\\mathbf a\\|\\,r\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\
\\partial_\\tau \\nu\n=\n\\nu-\\|\\mathbf a\\|(\\rho-\\nu).\n$$\n\nLet\n\n$$\n\
a_v(x,\\tau)=\\|\\mathbf a(x,\\tau)\\|.\n$$\n\nThen\n\n$$\n\
\\partial_\\tau(\\rho-\\nu)\n=\n\\bigl(\\rho-\\nu+a_v \
r\\bigr)-\\bigl(\\nu-a_v(\\rho-\\nu)\\bigr).\n$$\n\nSimplifying,\n\n$$\n\
\\partial_\\tau(\\rho-\\nu)\n=\n\\rho-2\\nu+a_v r+a_v(\\rho-\\nu).\n$$\n\n\
Since $$\\rho-\\nu=\\text{signedDensity}$$, this can also be written as\n\n$$\
\n\\partial_\\tau \\text{signedDensity}\n=\n\\text{signedDensity}-\\nu+a_v \
r+a_v\\,\\text{signedDensity}.\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\partial_\\tau \
\\text{signedDensity}\n=\n(1+a_v)\\,\\text{signedDensity}+a_v r-\\nu.\n$$\n\n\
A cleaner form purely in ``rho, nu, r`` is\n\n$$\n\\partial_\\tau \
\\text{signedDensity}\n=\n(1+a_v)\\rho-(2+a_v)\\nu+a_v r.\n$$\n\n---\n\n## \
Runnable Wolfram Language fix\n\n```wl\ndynamicRules = Rule @@@ \
dynamicSystem;\n\nsignedDensityEvolution =\n  D[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] \
->\n    Simplify[D[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] /. dynamicRules];\n```\n\nTo \
see it as an equation:\n\n```wl\nsignedDensityBalanceEquation =\n  \
D[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] ==\n    Simplify[D[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] \
/. dynamicRules];\n```\n\n---\n\n## Expected result\n\n```wl\n\
D[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] ==\n  rho[x, tau] - 2 nu[x, tau] +\n   \
anisotropyScalarFromVector[x, tau] r[x, tau] +\n   \
anisotropyScalarFromVector[x, tau] signedDensity[x, tau]\n```\n\nand after \
expanding ``signedDensity``:\n\n```wl\nD[signedDensity[x, tau], tau] ==\n  (1 \
+ anisotropyScalarFromVector[x, tau]) rho[x, tau] -\n  (2 + \
anisotropyScalarFromVector[x, tau]) nu[x, tau] +\n  \
anisotropyScalarFromVector[x, tau] r[x, tau]\n```\n\n---\n\n## Interpretation\
\n\nThis says signed density grows through three channels:\n\n* direct \
positive contribution from ``rho``\n* stronger negative drain from ``nu``\n* \
anisotropy-weighted amplification through both\n  * ``r``\n  * existing \
signed imbalance\n\nSo anisotropy is not just a passive descriptor; it \
actively modulates imbalance growth.\n\nIf you want, I can next give you a \
**drop-in replacement for your Section 7** that prints the balance law in \
both compact and expanded form.", "ChatData" -> 
